# PAM: enforce Google Authenticator TOTP for OpenVPN 2FA (cert + TOTP).
#
# Security-critical stack. The previous shipped config stacked
# pam_google_authenticator as `sufficient` followed by pam_permit — ANY
# wrong TOTP code (or none) fell through to pam_permit and authenticated,
# silently reducing the "2FA" to a single factor. This stack enforces:
#
#   1. vpn-pam-helper check-username — VPN usernames come from certificate
#      CNs and are NOT system users. Empty/invalid/traversal-looking
#      names are denied here (success=bad).
#   2. vpn-pam-helper has-secret — users created with require_totp=false
#      (no secret file) are allowed immediately (success=done): their
#      client certificate already authenticated them at the TLS layer.
#   3. pam_google_authenticator REQUIRED — everyone else (users WITH a
#      TOTP secret) must present a valid code. NO pam_permit fallback.
#      nullok is deliberately ABSENT: a user reaching this line without a
#      readable secret file fails closed.
#
# `user=root` + `no_strict_owner` reflect the container deployment: the
# openvpn-plugin-auth-pam runs as root and VPN usernames have no passwd
# entries — without user= the module's getpwnam_r() lookup fails for every
# VPN user (which is how the TOTP check was silently dead before, masked
# by the pam_permit fallback).
#
# Exit-code contracts of vpn-pam-helper are documented in
# scripts/vpn-pam-helper.sh; the data dir path is hardcoded in BOTH files.
auth [success=bad default=ignore] pam_exec.so quiet /usr/local/bin/vpn-pam-helper check-username
auth [success=done default=ignore] pam_exec.so quiet /usr/local/bin/vpn-pam-helper has-secret
auth required pam_google_authenticator.so \
    secret=/etc/openvpn/data/users/${USER}/.google_authenticator \
    user=root \
    no_strict_owner \
    noskewadj \
    no_increment_hotp
account required pam_permit.so
